  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1225 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nAs we head into the middle of the upcoming week, a definite \npattern change is in store, resulting in an increase in rain \nchances beginning mid-week and continuing through late in the \nweek. A strong mid and upper level low initially located in the \ndesert southwest early Wednesday will begin to gradually move \neastward into the southern plains states. Ahead of the upper low, \na plume of mid and upper level Pacific moisture will move into the\nregion, while the low-levels continue to moisten from the Gulf. \nWhile the operational models (ECMWF, GFS, Canadian) continue to \nshow some differences in handling the mid and upper level low, the\nensemble members offer a little more agreement. Overall, the \nECMWF Ensemble continues to be slightly slower in handling the mid\nand upper level features, while the GFS and Canadian are a little\nfaster. For now, it appears the chance for rain will noticeably \nincrease late Wednesday as daytime heating and more than adequate \nmoisture generate some scattered afternoon convection. An \napproaching Pacific cold front will then provide another focus for\nconvection, likely in the form of southwest to northeast oriented\nbands of showers and storms for Wednesday night into Thursday. \nDuring this period, there will be a risk of excessive rainfall and\nflash flooding. WPC continues to show levels 2 and 3 out of 4 in \nplace across all of south central Texas for Wednesday and \nespecially Thursday. Given the setup of deep moisture, a strong \nupper low and frontal boundary, this seems very reasonable. \n\nThe forecast for Friday will likely remain active given an upper low \nto our west, plenty of moisture and a frontal boundary lingering \nsomewhere near or just west of the I-35 corridor. Rain chances \nremain good into Friday, with the forecast for the upcoming weekend \nremaining highly uncertain. A widespread break from the heat appears \nto begin Thursday through the upcoming weekend with highs currently \nexpected to trend downward into the mid 70s to mid 80s.",
